// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
; specs/analog/mos_psp103_inv20.t27 -- the SAR ADC's inverter into 20 fF on the IHP SG13G2 PSP103 tables with the devices' own charge, judged by ngspice
; M5e of the analog emulation epic (#10486, issue #11350). specs/analog/accept_sar.t27 applies the external team's per-block checks to our models; its inverter block had one row it could not
; produce, "delays fall / rise into 20 fF" (the report's 81 and 100 ps, limit 1 to 500 ps). The 500 fF and 10 pF inverters of mos_psp103.t27 (M3b, M3c) are validated to 0.05 percent against
; ngspice, and said in their header that 20 fF is out of reach: the gate edge kicks the output node 5 times further than at 100 fF, past the 0 to 1.5 V range of the tables. This file is that
; run. The cell, the tables, the charge and the equation are the ones that are already there; what is new is the output node's freedom to leave 0 .. Vdd and the delay measure.
; -
; WHAT WAS ASKED AND WHAT CAME OUT. The issue asks for the two 50 percent delays of the SAR netlist's inverter into 20 fF on the tt tables with the devices' charge, the time step fine enough that
; halving it moves each delay by under 1 percent, a comparison with ngspice-47's own run of the same cell (a row PASS only inside the report's limit AND within 10 percent of ngspice), and a
; negative control with the charge removed. All four are here (ideal gate step; times in ps after the edge):
;                          fall (output falls)         rise (output rises)
;   this model, 1 ps       75.755                      93.246
;   ngspice-47, converged  75.368                      92.778          psp103_inv20_fine.cir (step 0.05 ps; 0.2 ps and 0.01 ps give the same two crossings to 1 fs)
;   gap                    +0.513 %                    +0.504 %        the bar is 10 %
;   the report             81                          100             its own stimulus; ours is 6.5 and 6.8 % under it: context, not the reference
; and halving the step: 4, 2, 1, 0.5, 0.25 ps give fall 75.971, 75.826, 75.755, 75.720, 75.703 and rise 93.485, 93.324, 93.246, 93.210, 93.191: each halving from 4 ps moves the delays (fall / rise) by
; 0.19 / 0.17 %, 0.094 / 0.084 %, 0.046 / 0.039 % (1 ps against 0.5 ps, the issue's 1 percent), 0.022 / 0.020 %; backward Euler is slow by its first-order error and the delay falls with the step.
; THE CELL. The inverter of the golden SAR ADC netlist: a PMOS of W 3.0 um, L 0.45 um, ng 1, m 1 with its bulk on the supply and an NMOS of W 1.0 um, L 0.45 um, ng 1, m 1 with its bulk on
; ground, supply 1.5 V, 20 fF on the output and nothing else. Where the repository states those sizes: the header of mos_psp103.t27 (THE CIRCUIT: W, L and Vdd, "facts of the circuit") and the deck
; psp103_inv_be_500f.cir (W, L, ng and m of both devices); the tables of mos_psp103_data.t27 are of exactly these two devices, so no table is new. 20 fF is the load of the report's row and of
; accept_sar.t27. The tables hold each device with its bulk on its source: here both sources are on their own bulks (the NMOS source on ground and bulk on ground, the PMOS source on the supply and
; bulk on the supply), so the source-to-bulk axis of M3e is read at Vsb = 0, which is the 2-D log read bit for bit (a test), and an end of a channel that goes past a rail is a forward junction, read
; at 0 as sar_comparator_q.t27 reads it.
; -
; THE MODEL. One node, the output, in words of 2^-24 V (voltages may be negative: f64). The equation is the backward Euler of M3c (newton_iter_q of mos_table.t27): the node holds C y + Qd_n - Qd_p,
; so a step from y0 with the gate going from a0 to a is
;   (y - y0) + KC (Q(y, a) - Q(y0, a0)) + KI I(y, a) / 256 = 0,    KC = 500 fF / C = 25,   KI = KC dt / 100 ps,
; a gate edge being a jump of charge (the Miller kick), not a current. The tables are M3c's: a word of a charge table is Q / 500 fF and a word of a current table is the current that moves a 500
; fF node in 100 ps, so no table is rescaled; C and dt enter as KC and KI (the integer step could not do it: a charge table scaled by 25 overflows the u64 bound of the bilinear read when scaled
; plainly and goes below 0 when scaled around its 2^21 origin, and `newton_iter_q` holds y to 0 .. Vdd in any case). What is reused, not copied: the current is `sq_i` of sar_comparator_q.t27 (the log tables on the source-to-bulk axis, the integer
; reader), the charge is its `sq_qt` (the tables read by M3a's bilinear read and extended linearly past their edges with the slope of the edge cell, so the charge and its slope are one function), the
; source terminal's charge its `sq_qsrc` (the two-edge closure of the tables' source terminal, Qs = Qd at Vds = 0 and Qs = 0 at a gate drive of 0, exactly), the tables `sq_qd_tab` and `sq_qg_tab`
; of its devices; the f64 here is the assembly of one node from them and the bisection that solves it.
;   the new part   the output leaves 0 .. Vdd: an ideal gate edge kicks it 0.40 V below ground when the gate falls and 0.50 V over the supply when it rises (ngspice 0.38 V and 0.46 V). Below ground the two
;                  ends of the NMOS channel exchange their roles (the drain is on ground and the output is the source: the gate drive is the gate over the output, Vds is minus the output, and the
;                  terminal on the output is the source terminal, closed by `sq_qsrc`); over the supply the PMOS's do. The charge and the current are continuous across the exchange (a test: 4 words and
;                  2 IUNIT over 2 words of output). The current beyond the tables' edge is held at the edge (`lim`, the reader's), the charge goes on linearly.
;   the solve      the root of the residual by bisection over -1.25 V to 3 V, 64 halvings (the residual rises with the output: a test), so the answer is the converged step, not K Newton iterations
;                  of the board's arithmetic; the DC rest level of each edge is the root of the current leaving the node. The gate is an ideal step at the start of step 0, the delay the crossing of
;                  0.75 V by linear interpolation of the samples from the edge at index 0.
; -
; THE REFERENCE. specs/analog/decks/psp103_inv20_fine.cir: the same two devices (mos_tt of IHP-Open-PDK 21133f21 through ngspice-47 and OSDI, 27 C) and 20 fF, a B source stepping the gate as
; u(time - t0) (no slew), the gate at the supply until 1 ns, 0 V until 2 ns, the supply from 2 ns, each level held 20 delays; trapezoidal rule, the step held to 0.05 ps. The reference's own
; sensitivity to its step is a number of the deck: 0.2 ps and 0.01 ps give the same 50 percent crossings to 1 fs (1.092778 ns and 2.075368 or 2.075369 ns). It prints the crossings, the rest levels,
; the kick (-0.384768 V at 0.56 ps and 1.96343 V at 0.49 ps after the edges) and 20 samples after each edge.
; THE WAVEFORM, the model at 0.5 ps against ngspice's 20 samples per edge: the rise is within 11.3 mV of ngspice's (0.75 percent of the supply) at the worst sample, the first, 0.5 ps after the edge,
; where the model's kick is 0.3958 V against ngspice's 0.3848 V (2.9 percent larger), and within 5.4 mV from 30 ps on; the fall is within 35.0 mV (2.3 percent) at the first sample, where the model's
; kick over the supply is 0.4984 V against 0.4634 V (7.5 percent larger), and within 5.8 mV from 30 ps on. The model runs 4 to 5 mV under ngspice's rising output and 4.5 to 6 mV over its falling
; one through the 50 percent crossing (5 mV at a slew of 12 to 16 V/ns is 0.4 ps, the half percent of the delays). The model's kick is larger by 2.9 and 7.5 percent; the cause is not isolated (the
; tables' gate-to-drain capacitance at a gate step of 1.5 V, their extension past the edge and the closure of the source terminal are the candidates).
; -
; THE ERRORS, kept apart. The scheme: backward Euler at 1 ps is 0.09 (fall) and 0.08 (rise) percent over its own limit in time (each halving in the table above takes half of the last: the limit is about
; 75.686 and 93.172 ps), which is a sixth of the +0.51 and +0.50 percent against ngspice: that gap is the tables' and the charge's. The extension: the same code at 500 fF and 100 ps (KC = 1, KI = 1, no
; table scaled) follows ngspice's backward Euler on the same grid to -0.0022 (rise) and -0.026 (fall) percent (psp103_inv_be_500f.cir; mos_psp103.t27's own log-table run is within 0.0018 and 0.025
; percent), where the kick is 6 mV and the range binds little: the new step is the validated one where the old one holds.
; THE NEGATIVE CONTROLS, tests that pass by seeing the failure (the bar is 10 percent of ngspice's delay):
;   charge removed           the devices store nothing, the node is the 20 fF alone: fall 39.043 ps, rise 51.868 ps, 48.2 and 44.1 percent under ngspice's. No kick, no capacitance but the load.
;                            Both are INSIDE the report's 1 to 500 ps, so the report's check alone accepts them and the cross-check is what refuses them (FR-004).
;   output held to 0 .. Vdd  the model with the bisection's bracket cut to the tables' range, as `newton_iter_q` holds the output: the kick is lost, fall 46.793 ps and rise 63.122 ps, 37.9 and 32.0
;                            percent under. The range is the first thing the issue says to extend, and it is why the M3c step cannot be reused as it is.
;   a load of 25 fF          the same model with 5 fF more: fall 85.544 ps and rise 106.242 ps, +13.5 and +14.5 percent over ngspice's: the bar sees a load error of a quarter; 22 fF gives +5.7 and +6.1
;                            percent, inside it.
; -
; WHAT A PASS SAYS. The inverter of the golden netlist, on the tt 27 C PSP103 tables of the repository with the devices' own drain and source charge, an ideal gate step and an output free to go
; 0.4 to 0.5 V outside the rails, has a 50 percent delay of 75.755 ps falling and 93.246 ps rising into 20 fF at a step of 1 ps (within 0.09 percent of its limit in time), within 0.52 percent of
; ngspice-47's converged run of the same cell, inside the report's 1 to 500 ps; the charge removed, the range held to the tables' and a load of 25 fF are each refused by the 10 percent bar.
; WHAT IT DOES NOT SAY. Another corner or temperature (tt, 27 C: the tables have no -40 or 125 C and the report's smoke suite is five corners at three temperatures; accept_sar.t27 keeps that line NOT
; MODELLED), another load or another input slew (the gate edge is ideal; the report's own stimulus is not in the material this file was made from, and our 81-and-100-ps counterparts are 6.5 and 6.8
; percent under the report's), the waveform inside the first 30 ps (11 and 35 mV off at the first sample), the current of a device past the tables' edge (held at the edge: the PMOS at Vsd up to
; 1.9 V and the NMOS at Vds up to 2.0 V are read at 1.5 V), the junction conduction of the kicked node (read at 0), the NQS and gate-resistance effects of PSP103, mismatch, noise, power, the output
; level at rest (30 nV here against ngspice's 0.64 uV, its gmin), hardware. This file is f64 with integer table reads and a bisection: it is the test-side model of the cell, not the board's arithmetic,
; and no board top, clock, LUT, DSP, BRAM, Fmax or UART line is claimed. The golden deck is not in this repository (its inverter is written out in the decks). ASCII only, English only.
; phi^2 + 1/phi^2 = 3 | TRINITY

module AnalogMosPsp103Inv20;

use analog::linear::{fabs64};
use analog::mos_table::{lg_fine};
use analog::mos_psp103::{HALF, cross_idx, rel_gap, steps_of_fs, INV500_BE_TPLH_FS, INV500_BE_TPHL_FS, EDGE1_500F_FS, EDGE2_500F_FS, DT_500F_FS};
use analog::mos_psp103_data::{TT_LG_N, TT_LG_P};
use analog::mos_psp103_gates::{GT_B_REL};
use analog::sar_comparator_q::{sq_i, sq_qt, sq_qsrc, sq_qd_tab, sq_qg_tab, sq_wf};

pub const KIND : str = "analog-cell";
pub const ID : str = "analog/mos_psp103_inv20";
pub const REFERENCE : str = "IHP-Open-PDK 21133f21 sg13_lv_nmos/pmos (PSP103) through ngspice-47, the inverter of the golden SAR ADC netlist into 20 fF (specs/analog/decks/psp103_inv20_fine.cir)";

; --- the circuit and the run ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// The load: 20 fF, in attofarad. The tables hold Q / 500 fF and a current that moves a 500 fF node in 100 ps, so the node equation below is scaled by KC = 500 fF / C and KI = KC dt / 100 ps.
pub const IV_C_AF : u32 = 20000;
/// The step of the verdict: 1 ps (a delay is 75 to 93 steps); the window of a run, 200 ps (past the 50 percent crossing of every mode of the controls).
pub const IV_DT_FS : u32 = 1000;
pub const IV_WINDOW_FS : u32 = 200000;
/// The supply in words of 2^-24 V, and the span the bisection searches: 1.25 V below ground to 3 V (the output goes 0.38 V below ground and 0.46 V above the supply in ngspice, 0.40 V and 0.50 V in the model).
pub const IV_VDD : f64 = 25165824.0;
pub const IV_LO : f64 = 0.0 - 20971520.0;
pub const IV_HI : f64 = 50331648.0;
pub const IV_ITERS : u32 = 64;
/// The modes of a run: the model, and the two ways of breaking it that the negative controls use.
pub const IV_M_NOCHARGE : u32 = 1;
pub const IV_M_RANGE : u32 = 2;

; A voltage in words (2^-24 V) is read into an unsigned word, rounded half up, inline: `(v + 0.5) as u32`. Every argument below is non-negative by the branch it is in (a gate is 0 or the
; supply; the output is on the side of a rail that the branch tests; a difference is taken in the direction that the branch tests), and a one-line function for it would be the body of
; `round_to_u32` of specs/igla/coder/arch.t27, which `t27c dupes --bodies` counts as a copy.

/// The source terminal's charge of a device of kind (0 NMOS, 1 PMOS) at the gate drive x (xn below the source) and Vds y, in table words: the two-edge closure of sar_comparator_q.t27 (its
/// `sq_qsrc`), read from the same charge tables, extended past the tables' edges as `sq_qt` extends them.
pub fn iv_qs(kind: u32, x: u32, xn: u32, y: u32) -> f64 {
    const pm : bool = kind == 1;
    const td : []const u32 = sq_qd_tab(kind);
    const tg : []const u32 = sq_qg_tab(kind);
    const qd : u64 = sq_qt(td, x, xn, y, pm);
    const qg : u64 = sq_qt(tg, x, xn, y, pm);
    return sq_wf(sq_qsrc(qd, qg, sq_qt(td, x, xn, 0, pm), sq_qt(tg, x, xn, 0, pm), sq_qt(td, 0, 0, y, pm), sq_qt(tg, 0, 0, y, pm)));
}

/// What one device puts on the output node: chg_w, its terminal charge there (table words, Q / 500 fF in 2^-24 V), and cur_iu, the current it takes out of the node (IUNIT = 1.164 pA; negative when
/// it puts current in). The field names are long and unlike any local of an imported spec on purpose: t27c's Zig backend gives an imported function's local the type of a struct field of the same
/// name (a field `i` of type f64 made the `i` of `cross_idx` a float, a field `cur` the `cur` of `sc_apply_lim`: the defect of section 7 of docs/analog/accept-sar-2026-10-20.md), and the spec did not compile.
pub struct IvDev { chg_w: f64, cur_iu: f64 }

/// The NMOS (drain on the output y, source and bulk on ground, gate at a; all in words). Below ground the two ends of the channel exchange their roles: the output is then the source, the gate
/// drive is the gate over the output, the source-to-bulk voltage is 0 (the junction is forward, as sq_dev reads it) and the terminal on the output is the source terminal.
pub fn iv_nmos(y: f64, a: f64, mode: u32) -> IvDev {
    if (y >= 0.0) {
        const x : u32 = (a + 0.5) as u32;
        const e : u32 = (y + 0.5) as u32;
        var q : f64 = 0.0;
        if ((mode & IV_M_NOCHARGE) == 0) { q = sq_wf(sq_qt(sq_qd_tab(0), x, 0, e, false)); }
        return IvDev { chg_w: q, cur_iu: (sq_i(0, x, e, 0)) as f64 };
    }
    const d : f64 = 0.0 - y;
    const x2 : u32 = (a + d + 0.5) as u32;
    const e2 : u32 = (d + 0.5) as u32;
    var q2 : f64 = 0.0;
    if ((mode & IV_M_NOCHARGE) == 0) { q2 = iv_qs(0, x2, 0, e2); }
    return IvDev { chg_w: q2, cur_iu: 0.0 - (sq_i(0, x2, e2, 0)) as f64 };
}

/// The PMOS (drain on the output y, source and bulk on the supply, gate at a). Above the supply the ends exchange their roles and the terminal on the output is the source terminal.
pub fn iv_pmos(y: f64, a: f64, mode: u32) -> IvDev {
    if (y < IV_VDD) {
        var x : u32 = 0;
        var xn : u32 = 0;
        if (IV_VDD > a) { x = (IV_VDD - a + 0.5) as u32; } else { xn = (a - IV_VDD + 0.5) as u32; }
        const e : u32 = (IV_VDD - y + 0.5) as u32;
        var q : f64 = 0.0;
        if ((mode & IV_M_NOCHARGE) == 0) { q = sq_wf(sq_qt(sq_qd_tab(1), x, xn, e, true)); }
        return IvDev { chg_w: q, cur_iu: 0.0 - (sq_i(1, x, e, 0)) as f64 };
    }
    var x2 : u32 = 0;
    var xn2 : u32 = 0;
    if (y > a) { x2 = (y - a + 0.5) as u32; } else { xn2 = (a - y + 0.5) as u32; }
    const e2 : u32 = (y - IV_VDD + 0.5) as u32;
    var q2 : f64 = 0.0;
    if ((mode & IV_M_NOCHARGE) == 0) { q2 = iv_qs(1, x2, xn2, e2); }
    return IvDev { chg_w: q2, cur_iu: (sq_i(1, x2, e2, 0)) as f64 };
}

/// Both devices on the output node: the charge (table words) and the current leaving the node (IUNIT).
pub fn iv_node(y: f64, a: f64, mode: u32) -> IvDev {
    const n : IvDev = iv_nmos(y, a, mode);
    const p : IvDev = iv_pmos(y, a, mode);
    return IvDev { chg_w: n.chg_w + p.chg_w, cur_iu: n.cur_iu + p.cur_iu };
}

/// The DC level of the output with the gate at a: the root of the current leaving the node, by bisection (the current leaving rises with y).
pub fn iv_rest(a: f64) -> f64 {
    var lo : f64 = IV_LO;
    var hi : f64 = IV_HI;
    var k : u32 = 0;
    while (k < IV_ITERS) {
        const mid : f64 = 0.5 * (lo + hi);
        const n : IvDev = iv_node(mid, a, 0);
        if (n.cur_iu > 0.0) { hi = mid; } else { lo = mid; }
        k = k + 1;
    }
    return 0.5 * (lo + hi);
}

/// The residual of one backward Euler step from y0 (whose node charge, with the gate it had, is q0) to y with the gate at a, in words of the node's own voltage:
///   g(y) = (y - y0) + KC (Q(y, a) - q0) + KI I(y, a) / 256,   KC = 500 fF / C,  KI = KC dt / 100 ps.
/// It is the equation of `newton_iter_q` in mos_table.t27 (the node holds C y + Qd_n - Qd_p; a gate edge is a jump of charge, the Miller kick, not a current), with the node free to leave 0 .. Vdd.
pub fn iv_g(y: f64, y0: f64, a: f64, q0: f64, kc: f64, ki: f64, mode: u32) -> f64 {
    const n : IvDev = iv_node(y, a, mode);
    return (y - y0) + kc * (n.chg_w - q0) + ki * n.cur_iu / 256.0;
}

/// One backward Euler step: the root of g, by bisection (g rises with y: a test). With IV_M_RANGE the root is searched in 0 .. Vdd only, the range of the tables and of `newton_iter_q`.
pub fn iv_step(y0: f64, a: f64, q0: f64, kc: f64, ki: f64, mode: u32) -> f64 {
    var lo : f64 = IV_LO;
    var hi : f64 = IV_HI;
    if ((mode & IV_M_RANGE) != 0) {
        lo = 0.0;
        hi = IV_VDD;
    }
    var k : u32 = 0;
    while (k < IV_ITERS) {
        const mid : f64 = 0.5 * (lo + hi);
        if (iv_g(mid, y0, a, q0, kc, ki, mode) > 0.0) { hi = mid; } else { lo = mid; }
        k = k + 1;
    }
    return 0.5 * (lo + hi);
}

/// The run of one edge into c_af attofarad with a step of dt_fs femtoseconds. edge 0: the gate falls from the supply to 0 (the output rises); edge 1: the gate rises (the output falls). The
/// gate is an ideal step at the start of step 0. out[0] is the DC rest level before the edge, out[k + 1] the output at the end of step k, for n steps.
pub fn iv_run(c_af: u32, dt_fs: u32, edge: u32, n: u32, mode: u32, out: []f64) {
    var a0 : f64 = IV_VDD;
    var a : f64 = 0.0;
    if (edge == 1) {
        a0 = 0.0;
        a = IV_VDD;
    }
    const kc : f64 = 500000.0 / (c_af as f64);
    const ki : f64 = kc * (dt_fs as f64) / 100000.0;
    var y : f64 = iv_rest(a0);
    out[0] = y;
    const r : IvDev = iv_node(y, a0, mode);
    var qprev : f64 = r.chg_w;
    var k : u32 = 0;
    while (k < n) {
        const y1 : f64 = iv_step(y, a, qprev, kc, ki, mode);
        out[(k + 1) as usize] = y1;
        const qn : IvDev = iv_node(y1, a, mode);
        qprev = qn.chg_w;
        y = y1;
        k = k + 1;
    }
}

/// The 50 percent delay of a run of one edge in picoseconds: the crossing of 0.75 V from the edge at index 0, linear between samples; -1 when the output does not cross.
pub fn iv_delay_ps(out: []const f64, edge: u32, n: u32, dt_fs: u32) -> f64 {
    const c : f64 = cross_idx(out, HALF, edge == 0, 0, n);
    if (c < 0.0) { return 0.0 - 1.0; }
    return c * (dt_fs as f64) / 1000.0;
}

/// The two delays of the inverter into 20 fF: rise_ps, the output rising after the gate falls (the report's "rise"), and fall_ps, the output falling after the gate rises ("fall"), at step dt_fs in mode.
pub struct IvDelays { rise_ps: f64, fall_ps: f64 }

pub fn iv_delays(dt_fs: u32, mode: u32) -> IvDelays {
    const n : u32 = IV_WINDOW_FS / dt_fs;
    var out : [2048]f64 = [0.0] ** 2048;
    iv_run(IV_C_AF, dt_fs, 0, n, mode, &out);
    const up : f64 = iv_delay_ps(&out, 0, n, dt_fs);
    iv_run(IV_C_AF, dt_fs, 1, n, mode, &out);
    const dn : f64 = iv_delay_ps(&out, 1, n, dt_fs);
    return IvDelays { rise_ps: up, fall_ps: dn };
}

; --- ngspice-47, PSP103 mos_tt 27 C, the inverter into 20 fF (specs/analog/decks/psp103_inv20_fine.cir) --------------------------------------------------------
/// The 50 percent delays after the ideal gate steps at 1 ns (the output rises) and at 2 ns (the output falls), ps, from the run with the step held to 0.05 ps (the .meas crossings 1.092778 ns and
/// 2.075368 ns; the runs at 0.2 ps and 0.01 ps give the same two numbers to 1 fs: 1.092778 ns, 2.075369 ns).
pub const NG20_TPLH_PS : f64 = 92.778;
pub const NG20_TPHL_PS : f64 = 75.368;
/// The extremes of the kick (V) and the time after the edge it happens (ps): the output goes to 0.3848 V below ground 0.56 ps after the first edge and to 1.9634 V (0.4634 V over the supply) 0.49 ps after the second.
pub const NG20_KICK_LOW_V : f64 = 0.0 - 0.384768;
pub const NG20_KICK_HIGH_V : f64 = 1.96343;
/// The output (V) at fixed times after each edge, and the same times as indices of the 0.5 ps run: 0.5 1 2 3 4 5 7.5 10 15 20 25 30 40 50 60 80 100 150 200 400 ps.
pub const NG20_IDX : [20]u32 = [1, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 15, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80, 100, 120, 160, 200, 300, 400, 800];
pub const NG20_RISE_V : [20]f64 = [
    0.0 - 0.384547, 0.0 - 0.380452, 0.0 - 0.367915, 0.0 - 0.355383, 0.0 - 0.342865, 0.0 - 0.330353, 0.0 - 0.299032, 0.0 - 0.267523, 0.0 - 0.203519, 0.0 - 0.138696,
    0.0 - 0.0738717, 0.0 - 0.00926922, 0.119042, 0.245808, 0.370431, 0.609224, 0.823863, 1.19920, 1.37848, 1.49742
];
pub const NG20_FALL_V : [20]f64 = [
    1.96343, 1.95850, 1.94441, 1.93024, 1.91603, 1.90178, 1.86579, 1.82891, 1.74982, 1.66483,
    1.57904, 1.49347, 1.32345, 1.15560, 0.991122, 0.681769, 0.425803, 0.102081, 0.0219727, 0.0000437790
];

/// The largest distance, in V, of a run of one edge at 0.5 ps (800 steps) from ngspice's samples ng (the sample j at index NG20_IDX[j]), from sample `first` on (0 is all 20; 11 is from 30 ps).
pub fn iv_wave_gap(edge: u32, mode: u32, ng: []const f64, first: usize) -> f64 {
    var out : [2048]f64 = [0.0] ** 2048;
    iv_run(IV_C_AF, 500, edge, 800, mode, &out);
    var worst : f64 = 0.0;
    var j : usize = first;
    while (j < 20) {
        const d : f64 = fabs64(out[NG20_IDX[j] as usize] / 16777216.0 - ng[j]);
        if (d > worst) { worst = d; }
        j = j + 1;
    }
    return worst;
}

/// The extreme of the kick of a run of one edge, in V (the lowest output of the rise, the highest of the fall), at step dt_fs, over the first 20 ps.
pub fn iv_kick(edge: u32, dt_fs: u32, mode: u32) -> f64 {
    const n : u32 = 20000 / dt_fs;
    var out : [2048]f64 = [0.0] ** 2048;
    iv_run(IV_C_AF, dt_fs, edge, n, mode, &out);
    var m : f64 = out[0];
    var k : u32 = 1;
    while (k <= n) {
        const v : f64 = out[k as usize];
        if (edge == 0 and v < m) { m = v; }
        if (edge == 1 and v > m) { m = v; }
        k = k + 1;
    }
    return m / 16777216.0;
}

; --- the tests ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Variables, not constants, carry the data of the asserts: an assert on a constant is folded by the compiler and counts as no assert at all.

test the_inverter_into_20_fF_has_its_50_percent_delays_within_1_percent_of_ngspice_converged {
    const d : IvDelays = iv_delays(IV_DT_FS, 0);
    var ng_up : f64 = NG20_TPLH_PS;
    var ng_dn : f64 = NG20_TPHL_PS;
    const g_up : f64 = rel_gap(d.rise_ps, ng_up);
    const g_dn : f64 = rel_gap(d.fall_ps, ng_dn);
    // rise 93.246 ps against ngspice's 92.778 ps (+0.504 %), fall 75.755 ps against 75.368 ps (+0.513 %): the same sign, a twentieth of the 10 percent bar
    assert fabs64(d.rise_ps - 93.246) < 0.002;
    assert fabs64(d.fall_ps - 75.755) < 0.002;
    assert d.rise_ps > ng_up;
    assert d.fall_ps > ng_dn;
    assert g_up > 0.0050;
    assert g_up < 0.0051;
    assert g_dn > 0.0051;
    assert g_dn < 0.0052;
    var bar : f64 = GT_B_REL;
    assert g_up < bar / 10.0;
    assert g_dn < bar / 10.0;
    // against the external team's report (81 ps fall, 100 ps rise, its own stimulus, context only): 6.5 and 6.8 percent under
    var rep_fall : f64 = 81.0;
    var rep_rise : f64 = 100.0;
    assert d.fall_ps < rep_fall;
    assert d.rise_ps < rep_rise;
    assert rel_gap(d.fall_ps, rep_fall) < 0.066;
    assert rel_gap(d.rise_ps, rep_rise) < 0.069;
}

test halving_the_step_moves_each_delay_by_under_0_1_percent_and_the_run_is_converged_in_time {
    const d4 : IvDelays = iv_delays(4000, 0);
    const d2 : IvDelays = iv_delays(2000, 0);
    const d1 : IvDelays = iv_delays(1000, 0);
    const d05 : IvDelays = iv_delays(500, 0);
    const d025 : IvDelays = iv_delays(250, 0);
    var one_percent : f64 = 0.01;
    // the step of the verdict, 1 ps, against half of it: 93.246 -> 93.210 ps and 75.755 -> 75.720 ps, 0.039 and 0.046 percent
    assert rel_gap(d1.rise_ps, d05.rise_ps) < one_percent;
    assert rel_gap(d1.fall_ps, d05.fall_ps) < one_percent;
    assert rel_gap(d1.rise_ps, d05.rise_ps) < 0.0005;
    assert rel_gap(d1.fall_ps, d05.fall_ps) < 0.0005;
    // every halving from 4 ps down moves each delay by under 0.2 percent, and the delay falls with the step (backward Euler is slow by its first-order error)
    assert rel_gap(d4.rise_ps, d2.rise_ps) < 0.002;
    assert rel_gap(d4.fall_ps, d2.fall_ps) < 0.002;
    assert rel_gap(d2.rise_ps, d1.rise_ps) < 0.001;
    assert rel_gap(d2.fall_ps, d1.fall_ps) < 0.001;
    assert rel_gap(d05.rise_ps, d025.rise_ps) < 0.0003;
    assert rel_gap(d05.fall_ps, d025.fall_ps) < 0.0003;
    assert d4.rise_ps > d2.rise_ps;
    assert d2.rise_ps > d1.rise_ps;
    assert d1.rise_ps > d05.rise_ps;
    assert d05.rise_ps > d025.rise_ps;
    assert d4.fall_ps > d2.fall_ps;
    assert d2.fall_ps > d1.fall_ps;
    assert d1.fall_ps > d05.fall_ps;
    assert d05.fall_ps > d025.fall_ps;
}

test the_waveform_and_the_kick_follow_ngspice_at_20_samples_after_each_edge {
    // the run at 0.5 ps against ngspice's 20 samples per edge: the rise is within 11.3 mV (0.75 percent of the supply, at the first sample), the fall within 35.0 mV (2.3 percent, at the first
    // sample too: the model's kick over the supply is 35 mV higher, 7.5 percent of the kick); from 30 ps on both are within 6 mV
    const wr : f64 = iv_wave_gap(0, 0, &NG20_RISE_V, 0);
    const wf : f64 = iv_wave_gap(1, 0, &NG20_FALL_V, 0);
    assert wr > 0.0112;
    assert wr < 0.0114;
    assert wf > 0.0349;
    assert wf < 0.0351;
    const wr30 : f64 = iv_wave_gap(0, 0, &NG20_RISE_V, 11);
    const wf30 : f64 = iv_wave_gap(1, 0, &NG20_FALL_V, 11);
    assert wr30 > 0.0053;
    assert wr30 < 0.0055;
    assert wf30 > 0.0057;
    assert wf30 < 0.0059;
    // the kick: the output goes 0.3958 V below ground (ngspice 0.3848 V) and 1.9984 V, 0.4984 V over the supply (ngspice 1.9634 V): the model's is 2.9 and 7.5 percent larger
    const kl : f64 = iv_kick(0, 500, 0);
    const kh : f64 = iv_kick(1, 500, 0);
    var ng_low : f64 = NG20_KICK_LOW_V;
    var ng_high : f64 = NG20_KICK_HIGH_V;
    assert kl < 0.0 - 0.395;
    assert kl > 0.0 - 0.397;
    assert kh > 1.998;
    assert kh < 1.999;
    assert kl < ng_low;
    assert kh > ng_high;
    assert fabs64(kl - ng_low) / fabs64(ng_low) < 0.03;
    assert (kh - ng_high) / (ng_high - 1.5) < 0.08;
    // the kick is real: both are over 0.38 V outside the 0 to 1.5 V range of the tables
    assert kl < 0.0 - 0.38;
    assert kh > 1.88;
}

test the_charge_removed_and_the_range_of_the_tables_are_refused_by_the_10_percent_bar {
    var bar : f64 = GT_B_REL;
    var ng_up : f64 = NG20_TPLH_PS;
    var ng_dn : f64 = NG20_TPHL_PS;
    // NEGATIVE CONTROL 1, FR-004: the devices' charge removed (no Miller kick, no capacitance on the node but the 20 fF): 51.868 and 39.043 ps, 44.1 and 48.2 percent fast
    const nc : IvDelays = iv_delays(IV_DT_FS, IV_M_NOCHARGE);
    assert nc.rise_ps > 51.8;
    assert nc.rise_ps < 51.9;
    assert nc.fall_ps > 39.0;
    assert nc.fall_ps < 39.1;
    assert rel_gap(nc.rise_ps, ng_up) > 0.44;
    assert rel_gap(nc.fall_ps, ng_dn) > 0.48;
    assert rel_gap(nc.rise_ps, ng_up) > 4.0 * bar;
    assert rel_gap(nc.fall_ps, ng_dn) > 4.0 * bar;
    // NEGATIVE CONTROL 2: the output held to the tables' 0 to 1.5 V range, as `newton_iter_q` holds it (the kick is lost): 63.122 and 46.793 ps, 32.0 and 37.9 percent fast
    const rg : IvDelays = iv_delays(IV_DT_FS, IV_M_RANGE);
    assert rg.rise_ps > 63.1;
    assert rg.rise_ps < 63.2;
    assert rg.fall_ps > 46.7;
    assert rg.fall_ps < 46.9;
    assert rel_gap(rg.rise_ps, ng_up) > 0.31;
    assert rel_gap(rg.fall_ps, ng_dn) > 0.37;
    assert rel_gap(rg.rise_ps, ng_up) > 3.0 * bar;
    assert rel_gap(rg.fall_ps, ng_dn) > 3.0 * bar;
    // and the model itself, on the same two measures, is inside the bar
    const d : IvDelays = iv_delays(IV_DT_FS, 0);
    assert rel_gap(d.rise_ps, ng_up) < bar;
    assert rel_gap(d.fall_ps, ng_dn) < bar;
}

test a_load_of_25_fF_instead_of_20_is_refused_too_the_bar_sees_a_quarter_of_the_load {
    // NEGATIVE CONTROL 3: the same model with the wrong load. The delay is not proportional to the load (the devices' own charge is 12 fF or so of it), but 25 fF in place of 20 moves
    // both delays by over 10 percent (fall 85.544 ps and rise 106.242 ps, +13.5 and +14.5 percent over ngspice's), so the bar of the verdict sees a load error of that size
    var out : [2048]f64 = [0.0] ** 2048;
    iv_run(25000, IV_DT_FS, 0, 200, 0, &out);
    const up : f64 = iv_delay_ps(&out, 0, 200, IV_DT_FS);
    iv_run(25000, IV_DT_FS, 1, 200, 0, &out);
    const dn : f64 = iv_delay_ps(&out, 1, 200, IV_DT_FS);
    var ng_up : f64 = NG20_TPLH_PS;
    var ng_dn : f64 = NG20_TPHL_PS;
    var bar : f64 = GT_B_REL;
    assert rel_gap(up, ng_up) > bar;
    assert rel_gap(dn, ng_dn) > bar;
    assert up > ng_up;
    assert dn > ng_dn;
    assert up > 106.2;
    assert up < 106.3;
    assert dn > 85.5;
    assert dn < 85.6;
}

test the_devices_are_read_at_a_source_to_bulk_voltage_of_0_because_each_source_is_on_its_own_bulk {
    // The inverter has no body effect: the NMOS source and bulk are on ground and the PMOS source and bulk on the supply, and an end of the channel that goes past a rail (the output kicked) is a
    // forward junction, read at 0 as `sq_dev` reads it. The read on the source-to-bulk axis (sq_i, the PR of M3e) at Vsb = 0 is the 2-D log read bit for bit, on both devices, at points over
    // the whole range of the gate drive and Vds, including the corners
    var k : u32 = 0;
    var same : u32 = 0;
    var total : u32 = 0;
    while (k < 25) {
        const x : u32 = (k * 1048576 + (k * 7919) % 1048576) % 25165825;
        const y : u32 = ((24 - k) * 1048576 + (k * 104729) % 1048576) % 25165825;
        if (sq_i(0, x, y, 0) == lg_fine(&TT_LG_N, x, y)) { same = same + 1; }
        if (sq_i(1, x, y, 0) == lg_fine(&TT_LG_P, x, y)) { same = same + 1; }
        total = total + 2;
        k = k + 1;
    }
    assert_eq(same, total);
    // and the axis is not nothing: at Vsb = 0.5 V the NMOS carries less current at the same drive (the threshold moves), so the equality above is a statement about Vsb = 0
    var x0 : u32 = 12582912;
    var y0 : u32 = 12582912;
    var vb : u32 = 8388608;
    assert sq_i(0, x0, y0, vb) < sq_i(0, x0, y0, 0);
}

test the_node_equation_rises_with_the_output_so_the_bisection_has_one_root {
    // g(y) of one step is strictly increasing in y over 0.5 V below ground to 2.2 V, for the two edges and for a step from the middle: the premise of `iv_step`
    var kc : f64 = 25.0;
    var ki : f64 = 0.25;
    var vdd : f64 = IV_VDD;
    const rest_hi : f64 = iv_rest(vdd);
    const rest_lo : f64 = iv_rest(0.0);
    const q_hi : IvDev = iv_node(rest_hi, vdd, 0);
    const q_lo : IvDev = iv_node(rest_lo, 0.0, 0);
    const q_mid : IvDev = iv_node(12582912.0, vdd, 0);
    var rising : u32 = 0;
    var total : u32 = 0;
    var j : u32 = 0;
    while (j < 168) {
        const y : f64 = (j as f64) * 262144.0 - 8388608.0;
        const y2 : f64 = y + 262144.0;
        if (iv_g(y2, rest_hi, 0.0, q_hi.chg_w, kc, ki, 0) > iv_g(y, rest_hi, 0.0, q_hi.chg_w, kc, ki, 0)) { rising = rising + 1; }
        if (iv_g(y2, rest_lo, vdd, q_lo.chg_w, kc, ki, 0) > iv_g(y, rest_lo, vdd, q_lo.chg_w, kc, ki, 0)) { rising = rising + 1; }
        if (iv_g(y2, 12582912.0, 0.0, q_mid.chg_w, kc, ki, 0) > iv_g(y, 12582912.0, 0.0, q_mid.chg_w, kc, ki, 0)) { rising = rising + 1; }
        total = total + 3;
        j = j + 1;
    }
    assert_eq(rising, total);
    // and the root the step finds is a root: g changes sign across it within one word, for the first step of each edge
    const y1 : f64 = iv_step(rest_hi, 0.0, q_hi.chg_w, kc, ki, 0);
    assert iv_g(y1 + 1.0, rest_hi, 0.0, q_hi.chg_w, kc, ki, 0) >= 0.0;
    assert iv_g(y1 - 1.0, rest_hi, 0.0, q_hi.chg_w, kc, ki, 0) <= 0.0;
    const y2 : f64 = iv_step(rest_lo, vdd, q_lo.chg_w, kc, ki, 0);
    assert iv_g(y2 + 1.0, rest_lo, vdd, q_lo.chg_w, kc, ki, 0) >= 0.0;
    assert iv_g(y2 - 1.0, rest_lo, vdd, q_lo.chg_w, kc, ki, 0) <= 0.0;
    // the first step of the rise ends below ground and the first step of the fall above the supply: the kick
    assert y1 < 0.0;
    assert y2 > vdd;
}

test the_charge_and_the_current_do_not_jump_when_the_ends_of_a_channel_exchange_their_roles {
    // At 0 V the NMOS channel's drain and source exchange and at the supply the PMOS's: the terminal on the output changes from the drain to the source. The charge is closed by the two
    // edges of sar_comparator_q.t27 (Qs = Qd at Vds = 0) so it does not jump: across 2 words of the output (119 nV) the charge of each device moves by at most 4 words (a word is Q / 500 fF in
    // 2^-24 V), for the gate at either rail; and the current is a clean reflection: the same magnitude on the two sides (to 2 IUNIT = 2.3 pA), the opposite sign
    var gate_hi : f64 = IV_VDD;
    var gate_lo : f64 = 0.0;
    var below : f64 = 0.0 - 1.0;
    var above : f64 = 1.0;
    var top_below : f64 = IV_VDD - 1.0;
    var top_above : f64 = IV_VDD + 1.0;
    const n1 : IvDev = iv_nmos(below, gate_lo, 0);
    const n2 : IvDev = iv_nmos(above, gate_lo, 0);
    const n3 : IvDev = iv_nmos(below, gate_hi, 0);
    const n4 : IvDev = iv_nmos(above, gate_hi, 0);
    const p1 : IvDev = iv_pmos(top_below, gate_lo, 0);
    const p2 : IvDev = iv_pmos(top_above, gate_lo, 0);
    const p3 : IvDev = iv_pmos(top_below, gate_hi, 0);
    const p4 : IvDev = iv_pmos(top_above, gate_hi, 0);
    assert fabs64(n1.chg_w - n2.chg_w) < 4.0;
    assert fabs64(n3.chg_w - n4.chg_w) < 4.0;
    assert fabs64(p1.chg_w - p2.chg_w) < 4.0;
    assert fabs64(p3.chg_w - p4.chg_w) < 4.0;
    assert fabs64(n1.cur_iu + n2.cur_iu) < 2.0;
    assert fabs64(n3.cur_iu + n4.cur_iu) < 2.0;
    assert fabs64(p1.cur_iu + p2.cur_iu) < 2.0;
    assert fabs64(p3.cur_iu + p4.cur_iu) < 2.0;
    // the reflection is not zero: the NMOS on (gate high) carries 33 IUNIT at 1 word of Vds
    assert n4.cur_iu > 30.0;
    assert n3.cur_iu < 0.0 - 30.0;
}

test the_extended_step_reproduces_the_500_fF_inverter_of_m3c_where_the_range_does_not_bind {
    // The same code at 500 fF and dt = 100 ps (KC = 1, KI = 1: the tables' own scale, so no scaling of any table) against ngspice's backward Euler on the same 100 ps grid (psp103_inv_be_500f.cir,
    // the delays that mos_psp103.t27's `q500_run` and `inv500_log` are held to 0.05 percent of): rise 1.34349 ns against 1.34352 (-0.0022 percent), fall 1.01804 ns against 1.01830 (-0.026 percent).
    // The kick is 6 mV there, so the extension past 0 .. 1.5 V matters little and the step is the validated one.
    var out : [2048]f64 = [0.0] ** 2048;
    iv_run(500000, 100000, 0, 60, 0, &out);
    const up : f64 = cross_idx(&out, HALF, true, 0, 60);
    iv_run(500000, 100000, 1, 60, 0, &out);
    const dn : f64 = cross_idx(&out, HALF, false, 0, 60);
    const ng_up : f64 = steps_of_fs(INV500_BE_TPLH_FS, EDGE1_500F_FS, DT_500F_FS);
    const ng_dn : f64 = steps_of_fs(INV500_BE_TPHL_FS, EDGE2_500F_FS, DT_500F_FS);
    assert rel_gap(up, ng_up) < 0.0005;
    assert rel_gap(dn, ng_dn) < 0.0005;
    assert rel_gap(up, ng_up) > 0.00001;
    assert rel_gap(dn, ng_dn) > 0.0002;
    assert up < ng_up;
    assert dn < ng_dn;
}

test the_rest_level_is_microvolts_from_the_rail_and_the_gate_low_rest_is_the_supply {
    // The DC solve with the gate at the supply (the NMOS on, the PMOS off) puts the output within a microvolt of ground (30 nV here, ngspice's 0.636 uV: its gmin of 1e-12 S is a leak of the
    // same order), and with the gate at 0 within a microvolt of the supply (0.45 uV under it, ngspice's 1.5 V at the 6 digits it prints)
    const hi : f64 = iv_rest(IV_VDD);
    const lo : f64 = iv_rest(0.0);
    var vdd : f64 = IV_VDD;
    assert hi >= 0.0;
    assert hi < 17.0;
    assert lo < vdd;
    assert vdd - lo < 17.0;
}
